hi there Steve Kaufman here  the linguist on language I want to talk  today about non-native teachers it's a  little bit dark here  non-native teachers of languages and  with particular reference to the  controversy which we have right now in  the state of Arizona in the United  States about the apparent decision of  the Board of Education there to ask  those teachers who teach English and who  have a strong accent or major problems  with grammar to no longer teach English  that those teachers should teach other  subjects or try to improve but they  shouldn't be teaching English if they  have a strong accent or don't or make a  lot of mistakes in grammar this has  caused a tremendous amount of  consternation amongst teachers and other  people who feel this is unjust and so  forth so I'd like to comment on this  from a number of different points of  view first of all I'd like to talk about  the role of non-native teachers and then  I will talk about this my view of the  situation in Arizona it so far as  non-native teachers are concerned the  vast majority of language teachers in  the world are non native speaking  teachers and I am quite sure that a  non-native speaking teacher teacher can  be just as good a teacher as a native  speaking teacher and nowadays there is  so much content available via the  internet and other places audio  obviously text was always there but  there is just so many ways in which a  teacher can bring the language spoken by  the native speaker to his or her  students that native the non-native  speaking teacher can be just as  effective and in some ways if the  non-native speaking teacher understands  the predicament  of the learner better that non-native  speaking teacher might be better able to  inspire and courage the learner so  speaking objectively I have nothing  against non native speaking teachers and  they are necessarily going to be the  main workforce around the world because  in most places where they teach foreign  languages that foreign language by  definition isn't spoken in the local  community so that's first of all now you  may know from my previous videos that I  believe that dependence on a teacher is  not a good thing that learners should  learn to become independent learners now  a native speaking teacher or a  non-native speaking teacher it can be  equally good in principle at encouraging  the learner to become independent and  that to me is the ultimate role of the  teacher to make the learner an  independent learner and I see no reason  why a non-native speaking teacher  couldn't do that just as well as a  native speaking teacher so that's point  number one  however because most teachers see their  role as that of teaching in the  classroom teaching the language rather  than cultivating and creating and  nurturing independent learners to the  extent that the teacher is teaching a  language there's no question that the  native speaker will make a better  teacher and the reason in my view is  this and that is that that a language is  different from other subjects like math  or physics or so forth it's different  because as I've said before in my videos  the attitude of the learner is so  important and a native speaker in my  view is a greater inspiration for most  people if I want to learn Chinese I'm  much more inspired by the idea that I'm  face to face with a Chinese person or if  I'm learning  hindu or polish or mongolian i don't  want to be face to face with an American  or a British person or you know Swedish  person I want to be face to face with  the native speaker that to me inspires  me that's stimulating I want to be like  that person I want to join that person's  language group so that's much more  motivating I cannot imagine that foreign  students who travel to Spain or England  or France or Germany or Japan to learn  the local language would accept to have  a non-native speaking teacher if they go  to Shanghai to learn Chinese they don't  want to be taught by an American or an  Indian or a Japanese so I think that  that there's generally speaking people  are more inclined to want to learn from  a native speaker now as I've said before  at what point do you need that  interaction with a native speaker in my  mind not very early there's lots you can  do on your own before you need that  interaction but when the interaction  comes along I think it's more of an  inspiration to be dealing with a native  speaker but a skilled native speaker  non-native speaker could perhaps  encourage the same kind of enthusiasm in  a learner I don't know but I personally  and I think a majority of people have  given the choice would rather have a  native speaker so that's the first point  that I would like to make with regard to  the situation in Arizona what happened  there was that Arizona has more and more  Hispanic people moving in some years ago  the government of the state decided to  bring in a lot of spanish-speaking  teachers they recruited them in Latin  America and they were going to make the  state of Arizona bilingual now they have  a different government as I understand  it and this government doesn't think  it's a good idea for the state to be  bilingual so they're trying to cut back  and the demographics in Arizona are such  that in grade 12 something like 12% of  the students are spanish-speaking but in  grade 1 45% of the students are  spanish-speaking so in other words there  is a bit of a language struggle taking  place in the state of Arizona which is  not unlike what I have you know  experienced in Quebec or what I read  about in Catalonia what I read about in  Latvia or Estonia where you have two  language groups struggling for power is  the state of Arizona going to become a  bilingual state or is English going to  prevail as the language of the state the  group that is in power right now that  were voted in demography in the  democratically want Arizona to be  english-speaking so that means that  anybody with the name Sanchez Lopez or  or you know whatever who arrives from  Mexico they would like him in the second  generation to be an English speaker just  as in Quebec which when I grew up  Montreal was 30% english-speaking the  government they decided that Quebec was  going to be unilingual French in order  to ensure the survival of the French  language they didn't want to become more  and more Anglophile and similarly in  Latvia where there are only a million  and some Latvian speakers in the world  they decided that they wanted to push  back and insist that any Russian  speakers who lived in Latvia should have  to learn the language so the idea of  these language struggles are quite  common but of course the reaction  amongst many of the sort of left-leaning  teacher fraternity to the situation in  Arizona is to call it all racist and  bigoted and stuff well to me it's just a  matter of democratic choice if the  citizens of Arizona vote in a government  that wants the state to be bilingual  they'll be bilingual if they vote in a  government that says we want the state  to be unilingual that's what they end up  with but in all of this what really  surprised me was  because I happened to be on this  listserv where English teachers comment  on different things was this to find out  that 25 percent of English as a second  language teachers in the United States  are non-native speakers 25 percent of  the teachers who teach immigrants  English are non-native speakers I find  that surprising not because those  teachers can't be effective within the  limits of what you can do in the  classroom and I think that there are  severe limits to what you can do in a  classroom but because I think that a  majority of people would naturally  prefer to have native speakers as  teachers whether justified or not that  would be the preference of a majority of  learners I think I could be wrong  certainly that would be my preference so  I was surprised to find out that 25% of  all English as a second language  teachers are non-native speakers anyway  there you have it I don't think the  situation in Arizona is racist  I think non-native speakers can be  effective but I prefer native speakers  thank you bye
 

